24 workers, 20 findings each — 480 expected

  shared list, no lock :  115 recorded, 365 lost
  same code, with lock :  480 recorded, 0 lost

The API latency is taken out of that deliberately, so the bug happens every
time instead of once a fortnight. With a real model call between the read and
the write the window is a thousand times wider, and the failure is the same
failure — you just meet it in production instead of here.

Six agents, all asked to make sure revenue for 2024 Q3 is on the board.
Each checks whether it is there before computing it.

  no lock                              6 of 6 actually computed it
  a lock around every write            6 of 6 actually computed it
  a lock, and a claim before the work  1 of 6 actually computed it

A lock fixes corruption. It does not fix duplication, because all six agents
read the board honestly, and all six were honestly told nothing was there —
the work had started but not finished, and 'started' was not written down.

The fix is not a bigger lock. It is claiming the task before doing it, so the
board records intent as well as results. That is the difference between a
shared variable and a work queue, and it is the point at which most teams
discover they have built a distributed system.
